## Status vocabulary (read side)

Projects in this tenant carry one of: `Deal`, `Open`, `Holding`, `Closed`, `Archived`.

The **active subset** = `Deal ∪ Open` (combined with `is_internal=false` where the
backend tracks it). Active projects appear in `master-cp.md`'s Active table; Holding
and Closed-recent are collapsed `<details>` blocks below.

`Internal` is **not** a status value — it's an orthogonal `is_internal` boolean on
the source-of-truth side, used to filter out internal pseudo-projects from the
master CP.

## Working tree layout (v0.3)

Project state lives in per-project working directories:

```
<tenant root>/
├── master-cp.md
├── weekly-cp.md
├── 1p/projects/<code>/cp.md          ← client engagements
├── firstpersonsf/projects/<code>/cp.md  ← First Person internal tooling
└── canonic/projects/<code>/cp.md      ← Canonic
```

Each working directory is a place for everything related to one project:
the `cp.md` itself, optional `_dropbox.md` link to media, plus any
hand-added text artifacts (transcripts, syntheses, action items). Binary
content lives in Dropbox per `.gitignore`.

**Auto-load:** if this session's working directory matches `**/projects/<code>/`,
treat `cp.md` in that directory as the primary context for this session
(equivalent to `switch to <code>` mode). The per-project `_dropbox.md`,
if present, is the canonical link to that project's binary artifacts.

When the cwd is the tenant root or any non-project subdir, behave as
described in "Reading modes" below.

## Local-link traversal (v0.5)

Each repo-source project working directory has a `_repo.md`. When that
file lists one or more `**Local clone (<User>):**` lines, the source
repo is checked out on at least one teammate's machine. To answer
"what shipped in this repo recently?" or "what's the current state
of `<feature>`?" without network calls, navigate to whichever clone
matches the current user (or any clone if running as a fresh agent)
and read `git log`, recent diffs, file contents, etc.

The mappings come from `<tenant-root>/.cp-engine.toml` under
`[local-repos.<user>]` sections — committed, multi-user, visible to
everyone on the team. Each user declares their own clone paths once.

A separate per-machine map at `<tenant-root>/.cp-engine.local.toml`
under `[local-repos]` (gitignored) drives `cp link-local` and
`cp capture-session` self-healing. That one is local-only.

Conversely, when the working directory is a tracked source repo
(`mc-2`, `cp-engine`, etc.) rather than a cp working dir, look for
`.cp-link` at the repo root. It contains the absolute path of the
corresponding cp working dir — `cd` there to read the project's `cp.md`,
session history, and decisions.

The `/cp-summarize` slash command (from the cp-engine plugin) writes a
session summary back to the cp working dir at the end of a development
session. Use it to keep the cp tree current without manual file edits.

